</p/><p><strong>Summary: </strong>
The start of a new age and a new campaign for a pair of knight tenants.
</p>
        <p>On <time datetime="2026-08-08" title="2026-08-08">Saturday</time> I ran a face to face game of <cite><a href="https://www.drivethrurpg.com/en/product/514996/mythic-bastionland?affiliate_id=318171">Mythic Bastionland</a></cite> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Mythic Bastionland”" title="Other site-wide references of “Mythic Bastionland”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-MYTHIC-BASTIONLAND">&#128214;</a></small>
 for two players.
The players opted to start as knight tenants (e.g., middle-aged knights).  We
had:</p>
<ul>
<li>Ser Denish the Tiger Knight</li>
<li>Ser Larkin the Fox Knight</li>
</ul>
<p>I asked Ser Denish what animals he had bested at their strengths.  He responded:
horses, wolves, and hawk.<small class="side-container">
  <span class="side-label"><span class="hidden">(</span>Sidenote<span class="hidden">:</span></span>
  <span class="side" role="note"> I secretly rolled a d6, getting a 3, and kept asking him to name an animal.
This left some ambiguity as to how many he would get.<span class="hidden">)</span></span>
</small>
 And I called for a <em>Spirit</em> check—which he
succeeded—to add elk.  In each case, I asked what their strength was and how he
had bested them.</p>
<p>For Ser Larkin, I read off the three available holdings and their woes, asking
them to choose which one he would be lord of:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dolemion:</strong> a fortress overseeing an isle of dolmens; with the woe of “unseen
revolt”</li>
<li><strong>Reint:</strong> a village with earthen banks, many gates, gridded streets, and a cage
for evil does; with the woe of “vast abduction”</li>
<li><strong>Cuseint:</strong> a tower with fierce defenders of an ancient language; woe of
“sanctioned killing“</li>
</ul>
<p>Ser Larkin chose Dolemion.</p>
<p>We talked through the roles of Ser Larkin’s council.  I recommended either the
marshal or the sheriff for Ser Denish.  The steward, responsible for overseeing
the holding in the lord’s absence, made little sense.  Ser Denish opted for the
sheriff.</p>
<p>Dolemion’s steward was the venerable Abron, the long-standing steward of
Dolemion.  The other roles—marshal and envoy—were vacant.</p>
<p>I then explained that the age of the Barren Coven had passed and we were now in
an unnamed age.<small class="side-container">
  <span class="side-label"><span class="hidden">(</span>Sidenote<span class="hidden">:</span></span>
  <span class="side" role="note"> To name the ages, I rolled on the Nature &gt; Land &gt; Character spark table and
a random myth.<span class="hidden">)</span></span>
</small>
 Both knights were married, Ser Larkin with two children and
Ser Denish with three.</p>
<p>I had the characters choose what action they would take as part of the turning
of the age: perform a great work or name a successor.  Both Ser Larkin and Ser
Denish opted to name a successor.  I quickly rolled up their children’s stats,
and by the luck of the draw both of their youngest children had virtues that
best suited them as successors.</p>
<p>Thus was knighted Ser Maldita the Willow Knight, successor of Ser Larkin.  And
Ser Torin, the Rat Knight, successor of Ser Denish.</p>
<p>I then explained the progression of time within an age; the three seasons within
each year and the three phases within each season.  Ser Larkin, realizing the
need for filling his council, opted to host a tournament.</p>
<p>I made the decision, that the domain could raise the required taxes, tithes, and
levies without issue.  However, any additional endeavors, that might draw from
those resources, would require action from the lord or council.  Thus, in
hosting a tournament, Ser Larkin would need to collect more taxes.</p>
<p>I had found rules for running tournaments, but found them inadequate.  When we
next run a tournament, for non-combat events, I’ll be using the auction mechanic
from <cite><a href="https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/348152/Whitehack-Third-Edition?affiliate_id=318171">Whitehack</a></cite> <small><a class="ref" rel="tag opener" aria-label="Other site-wide references of “Whitehack”" title="Other site-wide references of “Whitehack”" href="https://takeonrules.com/site-map/glossary/#abbr-dfn-GLOSSARY-WHITEHACK">&#128214;</a></small>
.</p>
<p>However, the highlight of the tournament was the joust—part because Ser
Denish opted to use his animal affinity to tilt the odds in his favor.
Tradition said that each knight, leading their horse, would shake hands in a
show of sportsmanship, and show honor to both knight and steed.</p>
<p>Ser Denish, with an affinity for horses, soothed the horse, providing a d8
advantage die for the first volley.  Which proved to nudge the results in his
favor.  But not without consequences, as his semi-final joust with Ser Maldita
resulted in a few things: first, Ser Maldita noticed something off about her
horse (succeeded in a Clarity check), Ser Maldita was scarred in the joust, and
ultimately lost.</p>
<p>I followed a strict order of progression on feat usage:</p>
<ul>
<li>Declare <em>Smite</em> and then make a Vigor save.</li>
<li>Declare <em>Focus</em> and then make a Clarity save.</li>
<li>Declare <em>Deny</em> and then make a Spirit save.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you failed your Vigor save you were not allowed to <em>Focus</em> nor <em>Deny</em>.  This
resulted in most victories coming from a dismounting (as they were rolling
d4+d10 and a possible d12).</p>
<p>Meaning Ser Larkin’s successor—Ser Maldita—knows that sheriff Ser Denish
cheated, and will almost certainly create complications in Dolemion.</p>
<p>From the winner’s Ser Larkin found a marshal and a wedded couple for envoy.
They also talked about how they might lift the curse that plagues the route
between Dolemion and Reint.  With the groundwork laid for the new age, we
wrapped our session.</p>
<p>I’m contemplating moving the calendar to the next phase of Spring; which would
put some pressure on collecting taxes.  And also lean into the idea of a slower
pace of life; which runs contrary to this modern life of ours.</p>
<h2 id="reflections">Reflections</h2>
<p>I’m thankful that instead of starting <em>en media res</em> I went with some up front
questions, drawing attention to some problems, and then letting the players pick
where to focus.  Also, in having a joust, with heavier armor, we were able to
play at the combat system.</p>
<p>And having re-read <a href="https://oldmenrunningtheworld.com/the-mythic-bastionland-gm-oaths/">The Mythic Bastionland GM Oaths</a>, I’m excited to dive into
more situation prep; namely the present and recent past of some of the knights
of the realm.</p>

